+#### Lighting Calculation
+
+### definition
+- [S4, unofficial] A lighting technique that calculates illumination for volume data using two types of light sources: floodlights (directional, uniform) and point lights (omnidirectional from a specific location).
+
+### step
+- [S4, unofficial] Floodlight Calculation: For each point in the volume, march in the direction of the light. Track remaining light intensity. If an illuminated area is hit, reduce intensity based on opacity. Stop when light intensity reaches zero.
+- [S4, unofficial] Point Light Calculation: For each point in the volume, march towards the point light source. Check for occlusions along the path to determine if the point is illuminated.
+
+### layout
+- [S4, unofficial] Light Shader: Contains intensity calculators for floodlights and point lights. Takes light parameters (direction for flood, position for point) as input.
+
+### usage
+- [S4, unofficial] Implemented in the 'vrc light shader' or 'lighting texture' creation step of the library.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of shading in volume rendering emerged to address the lack of depth cues in early direct volume rendering, which often resulted in ambiguous, "cloud-like" images [G]. Kaufman's work in the late 1980s and early 1990s introduced simplified intensity models and solid texture maps to highlight features for scientific interpretation [G]. The application of Phong shading to volume data became a standard technique for enhancing depth perception in medical and scientific visualization [G].
+The use of shading in volume rendering originated from the need to improve depth perception in semi-transparent data, which often appears flat or "cloud-like" without surface cues [G]. Early approaches did not provide any shading, but VanGelder and Kim introduced a fast technique to pre-shade the volume on the fly and then slice and composite a RGB volume to obtain an image with shading effects [S3, unofficial]. Meißner et al. provided a method to enable direct diffuse illumination for semi-transparent volume rendering, though it suffered from performance losses due to multiple passes through rasterization hardware [S3, unofficial]. Dachille et al. proposed a one-pass approach employing 3D texture hardware interpolation together with software shading and classification [S3, unofficial].
 
-Later developments focused on global illumination effects [G]. Illumination caching techniques were developed to pre-compute lighting to enable real-time performance [G]. Extinction-based methods using Summed Area Tables (SATs) allowed for efficient computation of soft shadows and ambient occlusion by approximating neighborhoods with axis-aligned structures [G]. More recent approaches, such as Next Event Estimation for emissive volumes, integrate path-tracing principles to handle complex lighting scenarios in participating media with energy consistency [G].
+The Phong illumination model, widely adopted in these techniques, was originally developed for surface rendering but adapted for volumes by estimating normals from scalar field gradients [G]. This adaptation allows for the visualization of internal structures and surface-like features in volumetric data [G].
+
+Modern implementations leverage GPU acceleration, with techniques like Illumination Caching [7548-illumination-caching.md] and Extinction-based Shading [9147-extinction-based-shading-and-illumination.md] pre-computing lighting to achieve real-time frame rates. Spectral methods like Doppler Volume Rendering [3536-doppler-volume-rendering.md] extend these principles to the spectral domain for scientific visualization of moving media [S1].
 
